      title: "medium.com"
    }
    summary: "In Tailwind CSS v4, manually toggling dark mode primarily involves using the `.dark` selector in conjunction with a custom variant definition and JavaScript to manage the theme state. This approach allows you to control the dark theme based on a class applied to an element, typically the `<html>` tag, rather than relying solely on the user\'s operating system preference.\n\nHere\'s a breakdown of how it works:\n\n1.  **Custom Variant Definition**\n    To enable manual dark mode toggling, you need to define a custom variant in your CSS. This is done using the `@custom-variant` directive in your main CSS file (e.g., `app.css` or `global.css`). This directive tells Tailwind CSS to apply dark mode styles when a specific selector is present. Common implementations include:\n    *   `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));`[1][2][3]\n    *   `@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *));`[4]\n\n    This code effectively creates a `dark:` prefix that applies styles when an ancestor element (or the element itself) has the `.dark` class.\n\n2.  **Applying the `.dark` Class**\n    To activate dark mode, the `.dark` class is typically added to the `<html>` element of your document. When this class is present, any Tailwind utility classes with the `dark:` prefix will take effect, overriding their light mode counterparts.[1][2][5][6]\n\n3.  **JavaScript for Toggling and Persistence**\n    You\'ll need a small JavaScript snippet to:\n    *   Toggle the `.dark` class on the `document.documentElement` (the `<html>` tag).[1][2][5][6]\n    *   Store the user\'s theme preference (e.g., \"dark\" or \"light\") in `localStorage`. This ensures that the chosen theme persists even when the user navigates away or refreshes the page.[1][2][5][6][7]\n\n    A common pattern involves checking `localStorage` on page load to set the initial theme and updating it whenever the user toggles the theme.\n\n4.  **CSS Custom Properties (`@theme` Directive)**\n    Tailwind CSS v4 heavily utilizes CSS custom properties (variables), which can be defined using the `@theme` directive in your CSS.[3][8][7][9][10] This allows you to define your color palette and other design tokens directly in CSS. When the `.dark` class is applied, you can override these CSS variables within a `.dark` selector, effectively changing the theme\'s colors. For example:\n\n    \n\nSources:\n[1] [tailwindcss.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGzyk4XUZ8Me62lyWqVYJOBl5AsVQPQGWRxKh4dZgH2yVMDs0lQ-p0EzSGWXr1mkCuuvSVRXXHeULgoGzJDtXzNl1G4mC7Bob8hMDcz7K_BCe0qcSFejhl5MHpz0yeIXv2u)\n[2] [preline.co](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGI9GgJlI3ctbo-Wl7ljI232gKJuLeKK-IwH0hrt6yTnKx1aVqphaseI9_0CTUQgSBMz4kMfZEcESRVVM6jZir3afYrM2pe_mgfof68iL-rVM5xaqTFFMf2SbR6a1Bu-vlu)\n[3] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE89JAuV7aZHAjMGXWSWWpgTDN406lgKSB_JWpQapKSxs6-m5vzwzzVhzswDShX0WNwGsUwzdQyAZmZXSEWJ2NIzSzAjQkDZlXfwFRC_aoBr8N7LXAEAfzKjfCg4mjIBglCkNuXPQZxRbb7ARFTKsHNvnKGel47BB2w1JCKHv-BAGDPkRiRye47h2DQm4EztrLAQkqRHwmLL9Gta894)\n[4] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFfX3bZxAQRg-vakVtuOQQZSvWMBiHd0lU0Wo0MeH4bm79hHbuEXBa9wNmSWGaUTdBpytVm2ppwP62Ubt_RgybFbBABvSxqggm6_lZ_Rw18hoTDOY6mcy5t9FrAOsT_OCwURRYwXLnn6S7r1FW9A6kZrZhL)\n[5] [djamware.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEswqiiIDhMiVDu8oFY90MrBT1XyIlWH823t3Qv_tGLipqDfrHrMoA4qcuF5f4F-n2_V9rYJgrvntKlqxEfAsozSsr7CiHzvhctlLUljvjiB68D_4P5uwgfJr2J197M0oc7m5EJseGPYKW99YxfTZHsr8fnetGhtDap4hrgeXoy9c3GJJrTfnPZKdIb)\n[6] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG27tJcefx-DmbAm0EGzeQd0qHpGNr3xISiSGvniqPP4-Mbp7HHruUF2UwU1aio5DpZz-ZOCboEgygT3Tg-I3ldI2UE0-LLIbg_KkfnAHNTRi1O4iyblsfP0h5Lc52tafw9pe5p2POJ0pQ8oCAfYXAl6_xm)\n[7] [weitzel.dev](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEqjdiF-iQVpZix7mp1txuY1RD6d_Zmw4Y-t