    thinking: "**Refining Aesthetic Considerations**\n\nI\'ve been examining color palettes and border styles, specifically `bg-white dark:bg-midnight-royal/60 border-royal-gold/15`. Digging into `LegacyPlanner.tsx` reveals that I\'m also considering the input fields and style declarations of these.\n\n\n**Analyzing Input Field Styling**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the nuances of input field and button styling within `LegacyPlanner.tsx`. The specifics of the `<input>` element are front of mind, particularly the impact of classes like `border-royal-gold/20 bg-white dark:bg-black/20`. I\'m paying close attention to the visual states. The buttons also have a lot to unpack, with a conditional approach to the border and background!\n\n\n**Clarifying Background Behavior**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the overall background changes in the app. My focus has shifted to the `html` and `body` elements in `index.css`, and I\'ve noted that their background isn\'t explicitly defined. The parent div of `App.tsx` handles the main background, but to prevent any visual discrepancies, I intend to set the default and dark background colors explicitly for `html` and `body` in `src/index.css`. I want a majestic look!\n\n\n**Defining Global Visual Consistency**\n\nI\'m now implementing explicit background styles for `html` and `body` in `index.css`, ensuring a seamless visual experience, especially during initial page rendering or system UI overflows. These colors should match the app palette perfectly. I\'ve also pivoted to examining the backdrop styling and ambient lighting in `App.tsx`, noting the use of `bg-royal-gold/5 dark:bg-royal-gold/10` to create a majestic glow.\n\n\n**Verifying Component Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now comparing the \"royal-glow-orb\" in `/src/components/Hero.tsx` in both light and dark modes. I am verifying that the soft gold glow, defined as `bg-royal-gold/5` (which is rgba(212, 175, 55, 0.05)), is working as expected within the context of the background and `mix-blend-screen` styles. I\'m focusing on the luminosity of this element and its visual harmony with the overall aesthetic.\n\n\n**Adjusting Blend Modes**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! Analyzing `royal-glow-orb`, I realized the implications of `mix-blend-screen` on a light background. It\'s washing out the glow significantly. I\'m now thinking about conditionally adjusting the blend mode based on the theme. Light mode requires `mix-blend-multiply opacity-25` or `mix-blend-normal opacity`, while the dark theme is perfect with `mix-blend-screen`.\n\n\n"
