    thinking: "**Refining Animation Details**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specifics of the ripple effect for the Bento Grid cards. I\'m focusing on ensuring the expansion is subtle yet noticeable to offer immediate tactile feedback upon clicks. I\'m also carefully balancing this with the overall design, ensuring the animation isn\'t distracting.\n\n\n**Expanding Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now integrating subtle parallax shifts to card backgrounds on hover, aiming for a more engaging visual experience. Additionally, I plan a staggered fade-in animation for Bento Grid cards upon viewport entry, leveraging Framer Motion for optimization. I\'m also ready to implement a CSS perspective-based 3D tilt animation on mouseover for the Bento Grid cards within ServicesPage using Framer Motion. I\'m focusing on ensuring these animations are applied thoughtfully across all pages and sections, prioritizing a refined user experience. I\'ll optimize my animations with an optimized React hook/tracker for the parallax effects.\n\n\n**Implementing Parallax Shifts**\n\nI\'ve just figured out a streamlined method for background parallax effects on hover. Using existing 3D tilt data from mouse interactions, I can now achieve dynamic background image shifts without external listeners. The `rotateY` value directly influences the X shift, creating a compelling depth effect. Similarly, I can inversely correlate `rotateX` with Y to complete the visual response. This approach is simple yet highly responsive, and should integrate very well!\n\n\n**Calculating Parallax Depth**\n\nI\'ve just determined the exact range for `rotateY` based on card dimensions, finding it spans from -25 to 25. Multiplying this by 0.8 yields a parallaxX range of -20px to 20px, and scaling images by 1.12. This creates a really awesome 3D parallax effect, and perfectly satisfies requirements for parallax depth, along with 3D tilt! We can combine Framer Motion and custom calculations to create the perfect tilt effect. We should animate the effect now!\n\n\n**Adding Ripple Effect**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a custom click ripple effect for the Bento Grid. I plan to use a sub-component within `ServicesPage.tsx`, managing ripple states directly. I\'ll store the ripple data in an array, tracking the id, cardId, click position, and size for each click. Then, I\'ll calculate the ripple size based on the card\'s dimensions to ensure full coverage. I think this approach should be pretty lightweight and performant. I\'ll also add a dynamic spring transition to the ripple effect.\n\n\n**Refining Custom Ripple**\n\nI\'ve just perfected a custom ripple effect for each Bento Grid card. I\'ve built a lightweight, performant solution directly within `ServicesPage.tsx`. The effect is completely contained and driven by a click event handler. I store ripple data (id, cardId, click position, and size) in an array and then render animated ripples with a dynamic spring transition, ensuring the ripple effect dynamically matches the card dimensions for full coverage. I\'ve also incorporated absolute positioning, and dynamic scaling to have an elegant and bespoke click effect. It\'s now perfectly sized, responsive, and uses a gold tint, with an extremely crisp animation.\n\n\n**Implementing Staggered Animations**\n\nI\'m now implementing staggered fade-in animations for both the \"Identity & Visual Strategy\" cards and the \"Content Kineticism\" Bento Grid. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent application across all sections of `ServicesPage.tsx`, specifically the Hero Section and Web Development sections. I\'m leveraging `motion.div` and `staggerChildren` to optimize the implementation. I\'ll focus on ensuring that these animations are all fluid.\n\n\n"
