ons like footers and dark cards. This approach fails to maintain visual harmony. The goal now is to allow the theme to work across all modes, based on matching themes.\n\n\n**Refining Color Inversion**\n\nI\'m now exploring a truly elegant solution: perfectly inverting the dark mode\'s color scheme for light mode. The key is to map dark backgrounds to light backgrounds (e.g., `#0F0F10` to `#faf8f6`) and adjust card colors accordingly. Border accents and text can also invert, maintaining aesthetic harmony. I\'m focusing on why a global yellow-to-purple and purple-to-white inversion was requested, and how to elegantly manage this inversion.\n\n\n**Implementing Theme Adaptation**\n\nMy current focus is on adapting dark mode\'s visual harmony to light mode. It\'s clear that the existing light mode has clashing text and background colors. I\'m now aiming for a matching color theme that mimics the dark mode\'s cohesive aesthetic but with light-colored equivalents. Specifically, I\'m identifying hardcoded dark backgrounds (`bg-zinc-950`, `bg-deep-violet`, etc.) and planning to make them adapt to light mode, like swapping `bg-zinc-950` to `bg-stone-100 dark:bg-zinc-950`.\n\n\n"
