  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Light Mode Colors**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `AboutPage` in light mode, specifically \"Card 2\". I\'ve noticed an inconsistency: the same hex code, `#2E1065`, is assigned to both `bg-deep-violet` and a CSS variable. This begs the question: are these intentional redundancies or an oversight needing clarification? I\'m digging deeper to understand the logic.\n\n\n**Investigating Color Clashes**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue within \"Card 2\" related to color overrides. The `text-royal-gold` class forces a purple text color, `#2E1065`, through an `!important` rule in `index.css`. This globally overrides text color to clash with matching background colors, such as `bg-deep-violet`. The simplest solution is to ensure dark-intended elements don\'t get forced overrides, especially in light mode, preventing color clashing in dark containers. I\'m now exploring `.text-royal-gold` and `.bg-royal-gold` for further adjustments.\n\n\n**Refining Override Strategies**\n\nI\'ve crafted a refined CSS strategy to tackle the override issues. My proposed solution targets specific containers and elements within light mode to maintain color integrity. I\'m exploring an approach that uses a `html:not(.dark)` selector to apply overrides only within light mode. Furthermore, I\'m specifically addressing dark containers to preserve the intended appearance of the original dark colors, ensuring consistency with the intended theme. I\'ve also identified potential enhancements regarding background color overrides.\n\n\n**Uncovering Overriding Patterns**\n\nI\'ve just made a significant discovery: the CSS selector `[class*=\"bg-royal-gold\"]` is overly broad. It incorrectly targets and overrides nuanced classes like `bg-royal-gold/10`, which should create a translucent gold background, but instead forces a solid deep purple. This explains the blatant color clashes and broken appearance I\'ve been seeing in light mode, especially with elements designed for subtle gold effects. It appears the general selector `[class*=\"bg-royal-gold\"]` is the fundamental source of the problem. I\'m now crafting a more precise fix.\n\n\n**Clarifying Style Conflicts**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the problematic CSS rules that cause color clashes. The overly broad `[class*=\"bg-royal-gold\"]` selector continues to be the primary culprit, corrupting elements designed with nuanced backgrounds and opacities, creating color inversions in light mode. This makes subtle effects into solid, undesired colors. I\'m now crafting a much cleaner, precise, and bulletproof CSS structure in `src/index.css` to fix this, starting with the pill example. I\'m taking the user\'s intent to switch text color into consideration, to match the light theme with the dark theme and avoid unwanted style conflicts.\n\n\n**Defining Color Variables**\n\nI\'m now exploring the utility of CSS custom variables to achieve theme-based color changes. By defining `--royal-gold-color` as `#2E1065` in light mode and `#D4AF37` in dark mode, I can manage color consistency and switch elements more easily. I\'m focusing on text-royal-gold, which will automatically be assigned the color according to the theme in use.\n\n\n**Revisiting Override Logic**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the initial rationale behind those `[class*=\"bg-royal-gold\"]` overrides. It seems they were implemented to handle specific dark mode scenarios where purple text appears on yellow backgrounds, requiring color inversions in the light theme. I\'m reassessing if those overrides are still the best strategy, or if theme-based CSS variables offer a cleaner solution for consistent color management.\n\n\n**Optimizing Specific Color Overrides**\n\nI\'ve refined the approach further, focusing on targeted overrides to address text visibility. I\'m now crafting CSS rules to specifically adjust `text-deep-violet` to white only when it appears within a `.bg-royal-gold` element in light mode. This leverages targeted selectors to prevent unintended global changes and maintain desired opacities. The goal is to avoid color clashes and unwanted style corruption, which are related to the broader selector strategies from before.\n\n\n**Implementing Targeted Overrides**\n\nI\'m now implementing the latest plan, focusing on targeted CSS overrides for `bg-royal-gold` elements. I\'ll ensure that any `text-deep-violet` inside a `bg-royal-gold` container automatically renders as white text. This approach is clean and avoids any global overrides. I also considered dark backgrounds remaining dark in light mode, such as `bg-zinc-950` with `text-gilded-ivory` classes, and how they should render. This is to guarantee there are no more clashes.\n\n\n**Addressing Low Contrast Issues**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the unreadable text in light mode within dark background containers. Specifically, the `text-royal-gold` class renders as a low-contrast deep purple on dark backgrounds, like `bg-zinc-950`. This poses accessibility issues. I\'m crafting specific CSS rules targeting these scenarios, ensuring the text color contrasts better against the intended background color. I\'ll test these fixes in the `ServicesPage`, `HomePage` and the footer, to be sure I\'m not creating more conflicts.\n\n\n**Refining Royal-Gold Styling**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial breakthrough, devising a brilliant strategy for consistent \"royal-gold\" accent styling. My approach targets elements based on their background: light elements will use purple as their accent, while dark elements will maintain their gold accent, ensuring flawless aesthetics and contrast. I\'m focusing on CSS selectors like `.bg-zinc-950` to maintain gold in light mode. This eliminates any color clashes by keeping consistent theme-based styling.\n\n\n**Finalizing Gold Color Management**\n\nI\'ve just finished implementing the new \"royal-gold\" accent strategy. Now, light backgrounds will consistently use a purple accent, and dark backgrounds maintain a gold accent. This is accomplished using a custom CSS variable, which dynamically adapts the gold color based on the current background, ensuring high contrast and visual consistency across the entire design, especially with elements like `.bg-zinc-950`. I\'m also now going to verify the implementation to ensure I didn\'t introduce any new conflicts.\n\n\n"
